//! check: fail(E0701)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: std.net, comptime.sandbox, comptime.sandbox.net
//!
//! **D33, held as a rejection: no sockets at compile time.** The capability
//! `std.net` carries (`net`, per I13) is refused categorically in the
//! comptime sandbox — the loudest case of the rule, because `wolf add` must
//! never mean arbitrary code talks to the network with the builder's
//! credentials. The diagnostic is `E0701` ("reaches the network, which
//! comptime code can never touch") and this file is its witness in this
//! repository.
//!
//! **Why the refused call is the BUILTIN and not the facade.** A witness
//! written as `comptime fn probe() { net.connect(…) }` does not reach the
//! sandbox at all: the comptime engine has no cross-module call at this pin,
//! so a `comptime fn` that calls ANY std module function is an honest
//! `unsupported` at resolve — measured on the pure helper too, where no
//! capability is involved (F-0051). So the refusal is witnessed one level
//! down, at the builtin `std.net.connect` delegates to, and the pure
//! `net.loopback` below is called at RUN time — which is also what keeps the
//! import used (an unused import is E0305).
//!
//! What the file therefore proves: the capability is refused at comptime,
//! and the two pure address helpers are the only members of this module a
//! comptime program could ever use — the day the engine can call across a
//! module boundary.

use std.net

comptime fn probe(addr: str) -> int ! {refused, timeout, io} {
    net_connect(addr)
}

fn main() -> !int {
    const FD = probe("127.0.0.1:1") else |_| 0
    let addr = net.loopback(0)
    print("addr: {addr} fd: {FD}")
    0
}
